-- title:
--   Transport of a Frobenius-rebased rigidification and multiplication by r
-- statement:
--   The setting is the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison between rigidified fake elliptic curves and Drinfeld's special formal modules; the data and hypotheses fall into the following groups.
--
--   **Arithmetic of the coefficient rings.** Two distinct primes $r \neq \bar r$ and a level $N$ with $r \nmid N$. A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal{O}$ which is a discrete valuation ring, an irreducible element $\pi$, completeness of $\mathcal{O}$ for the $\pi$-adic topology, $\#(\mathcal{O}/\pi) = r$, and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal{O}$; $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal{O}$. Further, a characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal{O}$ with an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, such that $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal{O}$ whose value at that element lies in this maximal ideal, every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree takes a value in this maximal ideal at some point, and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) - x^r$ lies in the maximal ideal for all $x$. Write $k_0 := O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$.
--
--   **Quaternionic data.** Rationals $a, b$ with `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`, that is, $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for each height-one prime $v$ of the integers of $\mathbb{Q}$ the completion $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b] \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ contains $r$ or $\bar r$. A $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule $\Lambda$ which is a maximal order (an order, maximal among orders containing it) and contains every rational integer, together with a map $\mathrm{coord} \colon \Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \times \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$ satisfying `IsOrderCoord`: additivity, $\mathrm{coord}(1) = (1,0)$, the twisted multiplication rule written with the Witt-vector Frobenius, injectivity, density of the image modulo every power of $r$, and the trace condition relating $m + \bar m = n$ to $(\mathrm{coord}\,m)_1 + \mathrm{Frob}\,(\mathrm{coord}\,m)_1 = n$.
--
--   **The base point over the residue field.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $k_0$ with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X_0$ of dimension $2$ over $k_0$ whose multiplication-by-$r$ series has kernel of degree $r^4$ (`hX₀`), two-dimensional formal coordinates $\theta_0$ for the structure morphism of $A_0$, and `hA₀`, asserting that $\theta_0$ is a system of formal coordinates for the relative group law of $A_0$ with formal group $X_0.F$ and that the $\Lambda$-action is computed on these coordinates by $\mathrm{coord}$.
--
--   **The special formal module and its moduli package.** A ring map $\iota \colon \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is special for the structure map induced by $\iota$ and whose multiplication-by-$r$ series has kernel of degree $r^4$. A moduli package $M$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is a Zariski sheaf, together with a family $\eta$ assigning to each ring $B$, ring map $\psi \colon O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ with $r$ nilpotent in $B$, and rigidified module $t \in \mathrm{Rigidified}\,r\,\Phi\,B$ a point of $M$. The hypothesis `hη` has three clauses: for noetherian $B$ and admissible $t, t'$, $\eta t = \eta t'$ if and only if $t$ and $t'$ are isomorphic; $\eta$ is natural in the ring, $\eta(t.\mathrm{map}\,f) = M.\mathrm{map}\,f\,(\eta t)$ for admissible $t$ and $f$ compatible with the structure maps; and every point of $M.\mathrm{obj}\,B$ becomes, over the members of a finite cover of $B$ by away-localisations at elements generating the unit ideal, the image under $\eta$ of an admissible rigidified module. Here admissibility of $t$ means that $t.X$ is special for the structure map built from $\iota$ and $\psi$, that $t.X$ has height $4$, and that $t.\rho$ is an isogeny from the reduction of $\Phi$ to $t.\overline{X}$ of height $4\,t.n$.
--
--   **Integrality of the centraliser.** A ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the set consisting of all $\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}\,a$ together with $\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$ into $M_2(K_0)$, assumed injective, and such that for some $m$ every matrix $A$ over $\mathcal{O}$ satisfies $r^m A \in \mathrm{range}\,E_0$ and conversely $r^m E_0(e)$ is the image of a matrix over $\mathcal{O}$ for every $e$.
--
--   **Comparison of residue rings.** A ring map $\kappa \colon k_0 \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ compatible with the two quotient maps out of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, an integer $n_0$, and a series $\beta_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is an isogeny from $\Phi$ to the base change of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ of height $4 n_0$.
--
--   **The point map $\Xi$.** For every noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$, every $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi \colon O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in $B$ and every rigidified curve over $(B,\psi)$ (a fake elliptic curve together with a rigidification relative to $A_0$), a $G$-point $\Xi$ of $M$, subject to four hypotheses. `hΞleg`: over a ring with only trivial idempotents the structure map of $\Xi$ is $\mathrm{frobTwist}\,\mathrm{Fr}\,k\,\psi = \psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^k$ for some $k \in \mathbb{Z}$. `hΞnat`: $\Xi$ is compatible with base change along an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\varphi$ when the curve and the rigidification over $B'$ are pullbacks of those over $B$ along $\varphi$. `hΞiso`: $\Xi$ takes the same value on two rigidified curves over $(B,\psi)$ whose underlying curves are isomorphic compatibly with group law, $\Lambda$-action and level structure, provided there are comparison morphisms $i_b$ of the $E_b$-parts and $u_A$ of the $A_b$-parts, over the appropriate legs, and exponents $i_1, j_1$ making the two isogenies agree after multiplication by $r^{i_1}$, respectively $r^{j_1}$. `hΞdef`: for $B$ noetherian with only trivial idempotents and with $\pi$ and $r$ nilpotent, and for a rigidified curve equipped with a formal module structure $(X,\theta)$ via $\mathrm{coord}$, there are $j \le 1$ and $t \in \mathrm{Rigidified}\,r\,\Phi\,B$ with $t.X = X$, with $t$ a rigidification transport of the given rigidification in the coordinates $\theta$ with parameter $j$ (the predicate `IsRigTransport θ₀ κ β₀ · θ j t`), with $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$, and with $\Xi$ equal to the $G$-point built from $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$ and $\eta\,t$.
--
--   **Frobenius and Verschiebung on the base point.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0^{(r)}$ over $k_0$ and a morphism $\mathrm{prA}$ exhibiting $A_0^{(r)}$ as the pullback of $A_0$ along the residue map induced by $\mathrm{Fr}$, morphisms $F \colon A_0 \to A_0^{(r)}$ and $V \colon A_0^{(r)} \to A_0$ over the base, each a homomorphism for the relative group laws (`F_hom`, `V_hom`), each commuting with the $\Lambda$-action (`F_act`, `V_act`), each preserving the level structure in the sense that points factoring through $\mathrm{lev}$ are carried to such points (`F_lev`, `V_lev`), with $V \circ F$ and $F \circ V$ equal to multiplication by $r$ on points (`V_F`, `F_V`), and with `F_frob`: for every commutative ring $C$ of characteristic $r$ and every $x \colon \mathrm{Spec}\,C \to A_0$, the composite $x$ followed by $F$ followed by $\mathrm{prA}$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the $r$-power Frobenius of $C$ followed by $x$.
--
--   **The test object.** A noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$ with only trivial idempotents, an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra map $\psi \colon O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in $B$, a fake elliptic curve $E$ over $B$, a rigidification $\rho$ of $E$ relative to $\psi$ and a rigidification $\rho^{+}$ of the same $E$ relative to $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}$, linked by: a morphism $u_b \colon \rho.E_b \to \rho^{+}.E_b$ with $u_b$ followed by $\rho^{+}.g_b$ equal to $\rho.g_b$ and $u_b$ over the base; a morphism $g_A'$ exhibiting $\rho^{+}.A_b$ as the pullback of $A_0^{(r)}$ along the residue map of $\psi$, with $g_A'$ followed by $\mathrm{prA}$ equal to $\rho^{+}.g_A$; morphisms $F_b \colon \rho.A_b \to \rho^{+}.A_b$ and $V_b \colon \rho^{+}.A_b \to \rho.A_b$ over the base with $F_b$ followed by $g_A'$ equal to $\rho.g_A$ followed by $F$, and $V_b$ followed by $\rho.g_A$ equal to $g_A'$ followed by $V$; the degree shift $\rho^{+}.d = \rho.d + 1$; and the two compatibilities $u_b \gg \rho^{+}.\varphi = \rho.\varphi \gg F_b$ and $\rho^{+}.\varphi' = V_b \gg \rho.\varphi' \gg u_b$ (written in diagrammatic order). Finally a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X$ over $B$ and formal coordinates $\theta$ for $E$ with `hX` asserting that $E$ is a formal module via $\mathrm{coord}$, $X$, $\theta$, a natural number $j'$ and a rigidified module $t'$ over $B$ with $t'.X = X$, with all constant coefficients of the series $t'.\rho$ equal to zero, and with $t'$ a transport of $\rho^{+}$ in the coordinates $\theta$ with parameter $j'$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exists $t'' \in \mathrm{Rigidified}\,r\,\Phi\,B$ such that: $t''.X = t'.X$; every component of the series $t''.\rho$ has vanishing constant coefficient; $t''$ is a transport of the original rigidification $\rho$ in the same coordinates $\theta$ and with the same parameter $j'$, namely `IsRigTransport θ₀ κ β₀ ρ θ j' t''`, which unfolds to the existence of a ring map $\kappa_B \colon B/(\pi) \to B/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps out of $B$ and with $\kappa$ along the residue legs of $\psi$, and of a series $\sigma$ over $B/(\pi)$ which represents the quasi-isogeny $\rho.\varphi'$ followed by $\rho.g_b$ on formal coordinates (for every test algebra over $B/(\pi)$, $B$ and $k_0$ with compatible structure maps, every nilpotent ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1} = 0$, every tuple $s$ with entries in $J$ and every point $P_A$ of $\rho.A_b$ over that algebra lying over $\theta_0(s)$ through $\rho.g_A$, the composite of $P_A$ with $\rho.\varphi'$ and $\rho.g_b$ equals $\theta$ evaluated at the truncated values of $\sigma$ at $s$), such that $t''.\rho$ is obtained from the reduction of $\sigma$ along $\kappa_B$ by substituting the reduction of $\beta_0$ along the residue map of $\psi$, itself precomposed with the substitution $X_i \mapsto X_i^{r^{j'}}$; and, lastly, the Frobenius relation
--   $$t'.\rho \circ \mathrm{frobSeries}_1 \;=\; [r]_{t''.\overline{X}} \circ t''.\rho,$$
--   that is, substituting $X_i \mapsto X_i^{r}$ into each component of $t'.\rho$ gives the same series over $B/(r)$ as substituting $t''.\rho$ into the series describing the action of $r \in \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$ on the reduction $t''.\overline{X}$ of $t''.X$ modulo $r$.
--
--   No assertion is made about the integer $t''.n$, nor about admissibility of $t''$.
--
--   This is the comparison step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld dictionary which relates the transport of a rigidification to the transport of its rebasing along the Frobenius automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$ of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$: the two transports can be chosen with the same formal module, the same coordinates and the same parameter $j'$, and then differ by Frobenius on one side and multiplication by $r$ on the other. It is used to pin down the level datum $n$ of a transport and to show that the induced map to the moduli package changes by a scalar power of the uniformiser when the structure map is twisted by $\mathrm{Fr}$.
